p": [[" the", -0.2337706834077835], [" loading", -1.8745254278182983]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.08325811475515366, "top": [[" driver", -0.08325811475515366], [" module", -2.964787006378174]]}, {"t": " loads", "lp": -0.22014233469963074, "top": [[" loads", -0.22014233469963074], [" initializes", -1.6336040496826172]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.7190291881561279, "top": [["\n", -0.7190291881561279], [" (", -1.091688871383667]]}, {"t": "-", "lp": -0.0003340802213642746, "top": [["-", -0.0003340802213642746], [".", -10.872812271118164]]}, {"t": " Intel", "lp": -0.5591238141059875, "top": [[" Intel", -0.5591238141059875], [" The", -1.490075945854187]]}, {"t": "'s", "lp": -0.12553101778030396, "top": [["'s", -0.12553101778030396], [" Arc", -2.9702556133270264]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" a CCS from another GPU context when your context needs one but none are available\n- More flexible but can cause contention/preemption issues between contexts\n- Generally the safer default for most workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **No stealing**: The driver will not steal CCS from other contexts\n- If no CCS is available, your context may fail or fall back\n- More predictable behavior but can hit resource limits sooner\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- Typically relates to **how CCS allocation/fallback** is handled in newer driver versions\n- Often used for specific **compute-heavy workloads** where you want explicit control over CCS assignment\n\n---\n\n## When to use which\n\n| Mode | Best for |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | General use, most stable default |\n| **2** | When you want deterministic behavior and have enough CCS available |\n| **4** | Specialized compute workloads, testing, or when directed by driver documentation |\n\n---\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f **Important caveat**: CCS mode behavior has changed across driver versions, and the exact semantics of mode 4 specifically aren't consistently documented. The numbering and meaning can differ between:\n- Kernel `xe` driver versions\n- User-space `libva`/`onevpl` versions\n- Firmware versions\n\n**My recommendation**: Check your specific driver version's documentation. You can find the current meaning in:\n- The kernel source: `Documentation/gpu/xe.rst` or similar\n- `dmesg` output when the driver loads\n- Intel's"}